msgid "The swarm deploy handler includes it for every network `lookup('stream_relay_networks')` returns, on the reverse proxy and on the provider, so whichever stack deploys first finds the network. The handler runs the lookup only in swarm, so a compose flush pays nothing for it. A relay network belongs to no role network: its name is `<entity>-relay`, its subnet is the provider's `overlay.stream_relay.subnet` from the role subnet pool, and it carries the stack namespace of the provider, so `docker stack rm <entity>` removes it with the provider's stack. A network of that name that is not an attachable swarm overlay on that subnet, with the configured encryption and namespace, fails the deploy; a probe that fails fails it too. Encryption is the presence of the `encrypted` option, whatever its value, so the option is left out when `swarm.network.encryption` is off."
msgstr ""

msgid "The swarm deploy handler pre-creates the relay network for both stacks, so whichever of openldap and openresty deploys first finds it, and it runs that lookup only in swarm, so a compose flush pays nothing for it. The relay network is an attachable overlay on the pinned `stream_relay.subnet` (`192.168.105.48/28`, from the /28 block of the role subnet pool), which the allocator, the pool lint and the overlap test see like a `local.subnet`. It carries the `com.docker.compose.network`, `com.docker.compose.project` and `com.docker.stack.namespace` labels of the provider's stack, so `docker stack rm openldap` removes it once openresty has left it; until then, switching the relay off leaves the empty network in place."
